.TH RECOVER 1 "2026-09-19" "recover 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
recover \- record commands and their output, then find them again later
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B r
.I "command ..."
.br
.B recover run
.RB [ \-c ]
.RI [ -- ]
.I "command ..."
.br
.B recover
.RI [ filters ]
.br
.B recover
.I id
.RB [ out | cmd | com | show | rerun | rm ]
.RI [ args ]
.br
.B recover
.RB { out | cmd | show | rerun | rm }
.I id
.br
.B recover
.RB { export | import | clear | db " " path | help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shell history keeps the command but throws away the output, exit code, and timing.
.B recover
opts a command into recording with a short prefix \(em
.B r echo test
\(em running it transparently (you still see live output) while saving the command, its
combined stdout+stderr, exit code, timestamps, and duration into a single portable SQLite
file. A taskwarrior\-style query CLI then lists, regex\-searches, and date\-filters those runs.
.PP
The command runs inside a pseudo\-terminal, so colors, progress bars, and interactive TUIs
behave as in a normal shell. Output is stored verbatim (binary\-safe); ANSI escapes are
stripped only for regex matching and for
.BR "out \-\-strip" .
Run ids are stable: a run's number never changes.
.SH RECORDING
.TP
.B r \fIcommand ...\fR
Run and record a command. Installed as a shell function:
.RS
.nf
r() { recover run "$@"; }
.fi
.RE
For a command whose name starts with a dash, use \fBrecover run \-\- \fIcommand\fR.
.TP
.B recover run \fR[\fB\-c\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fIcommand ...\fR
The underlying recorder.
.B \-c
runs the arguments as a shell pipeline via
.BR $SHELL " " \-c
(quote it), e.g. \fBrecover run \-c 'make 2>&1 | tail'\fR. Without
.BR \-c ,
the argument vector is executed directly. A pipeline typed unquoted
(\fBr echo x | grep x\fR) records only the first stage \(em your interactive shell owns the
pipe before \fBr\fR sees it; quote it or use \fB\-c\fR.
.SH QUERYING
Every run action can be written id\-first (\fBrecover 42 out\fR) or verb\-first
(\fBrecover out 42\fR).
.TP
.B recover \fR[\fIfilters\fR]
List matching runs, newest first (default limit 10). With no reserved word, bare
\fIkey:value\fR tokens imply this listing.
.TP
.B recover \fIid\fR
Show a run in full (metadata, note, and output).
.TP
.B recover \fIid\fR out\fR, \fBrecover out \fIid\fR \fR[\fB\-\-strip\fR]
Print the run's stored output. With
.B \-\-strip
ANSI escapes are removed and CRLF is normalized. Raw output is byte\-for\-byte otherwise, so
it pipes onward: \fBrecover 42 out | grep panic\fR.
.TP
.B recover \fIid\fR cmd\fR, \fBrecover cmd \fIid\fR
Print just the command text.
.TP
.B recover \fIid\fR com\fR, \fBrecover \fIid\fB com "\fItext\fB"
With no text, print the run's note. With text, set (or, if empty, clear) the note. Notes mark
important runs: they appear after the command in the colored list when there is room, and are
searchable with the \fBcom:\fR filter.
.TP
.B recover \fIid\fR show\fR, \fBrecover show \fIid\fR
Print full detail for a run.
.TP
.B recover \fIid\fR rerun\fR, \fBrecover rerun \fIid\fR
Re\-execute the stored command (through \fB$SHELL \-c\fR), recording a new run.
.TP
.B recover \fIid\fR rm\fR, \fBrecover rm \fIid\fR
Delete a run. \fBrecover clear \-\-yes\fR deletes all runs.
.SH RUN IDS
A positive id is the run's stable, permanent number. A negative id counts back from the
newest run: \fB\-1\fR is the most recent, \fB\-2\fR the one before, and so on \(em so you can
act on a run without listing first. Negative ids are a quick\-mode feature (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.SH FILTERS
Filters are taskwarrior\-style \fIkey:value\fR tokens, combined with AND. Cheap predicates
(date, exit code, limit) run in SQL; regex predicates run against the command and the
ANSI\-stripped output.
.TP
.BI cmd: regex
Regex over the command text.
.TP
.BI out: regex
Regex over the output.
.TP
.BI reg: regex
Regex over the command OR the output.
.TP
.BI com: regex
Regex over the note.
.TP
.BI date: expr "\fR, \fB" date: A .. B "\fR, \fB" date.after: expr "\fR, \fB" date.before: expr
Date bounds. \fBdate:\fIX\fR means at\-or\-after \fIX\fR (a window); \fBdate:\fIA\fB..\fIB\fR
is the half\-open range [\fIA\fR, \fIB\fR); the one\-sided forms bound below/above.
.TP
.BI exit: code
Only runs with this exit code.
.TP
.BI limit: n
Cap the number of results (\fBlimit:0\fR means no cap; default 10).
.PP
Regex is Rust
.BR regex (7)
syntax, case\-sensitive by default \(em use \fB(?i)\fR for insensitive.
.SH DATE EXPRESSIONS
Anchors: \fBnow\fR, \fBtoday\fR, \fByesterday\fR, \fBtomorrow\fR, \fBYYYY-MM-DD\fR, and RFC3339
timestamps. An anchor may carry one arithmetic term, \fIanchor\fR\fB\(+-\fIN\fBunit\fR, with
units \fBs min h day week month year\fR \(em e.g. \fBtoday-5day\fR, \fBnow-2h\fR,
\fBtoday+1week\fR.
.PP
A bare duration with no anchor is a quick\-mode shorthand for minus\-from\-default: day/week/
month/year units count from \fBtoday\fR (midnight), while second/minute/hour units count from
\fBnow\fR. So \fBdate:3day\fR == \fBdate:today-3day\fR, but \fBdate:2h\fR == \fBdate:now-2h\fR.
Use \fB+\fR for the future (\fBdate:+3day\fR).
.SH EXPORT AND IMPORT
.TP
.B recover export \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fIFILE\fR]
Write all runs as JSON (to \fIFILE\fR, else stdout). Output is base64\-encoded to stay lossless.
.TP
.B recover import \fIFILE\fR
Load runs from a JSON export, appending them.
.TP
.B recover db path
Print the database file path (copy that one file to move your history).
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings live in \fB~/.recoverc\fR (override the path with \fBRECOVER_CONFIG\fR). The file is
optional. Format is \fIkey = value\fR, one per line, with \fB#\fR full\-line comments.
.TP
.B mode = quick|standard
\fBquick\fR (default) enables the shortcuts: negative indices and bare\-duration dates.
\fBstandard\fR disables them (positive ids and explicit anchors only). Does not affect colors.
.TP
.B color = auto|on|off
Colorize the list. \fBauto\fR (default) colors only on a terminal; \fBon\fR forces it;
\fBoff\fR disables it. The \fBNO_COLOR\fR environment variable always wins.
.TP
.B bar_color = #rrggbb
Zebra\-stripe background (hex). Default \fB#000033\fR. Text on the stripe is auto\-chosen
(black or white) for contrast.
.TP
.B header_color = #rrggbb
Header text color (hex). Omit for bold only.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RECOVER_DB
Path to the database file. Default \fB$XDG_DATA_HOME/recover/recover.db\fR, else
\fB~/.local/share/recover/recover.db\fR.
.TP
.B RECOVER_CONFIG
Path to the config file. Default \fB~/.recoverc\fR.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, disables colored output regardless of the \fBcolor\fR setting.
.TP
.B SHELL
Used by \fB\-c\fR and \fBrerun\fR to run shell command lines. Default \fB/bin/sh\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.local/share/recover/recover.db
Default database (a single SQLite file).
.TP
.B ~/.recoverc
Configuration file.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Record a command and inspect it:
.RS
.nf
r cargo build
recover                 # list recent runs
recover \-1 out          # output of the run you just made
recover \-1 com "green"  # flag it
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find things later:
.RS
.nf
recover reg:panic date:today-5day
recover cmd:'^git' exit:1
recover com:important
recover date:14day..7day          # last week, excluding this week
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B recover run
exits with the recorded command's exit code. Query commands exit 0 on success and non\-zero
on error (e.g. an unknown run id or an invalid filter).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sqlite3 (1),
.BR regex (7)
.SH AUTHOR
Written with the assistance of Claude Code.
